{-| Proto3 JSON decoders, the counterpart of `Json.Decode` for messages: what
code generated by elm-connect calls to read a message. An application runs a
generated decoder, such as `Notes.V1.decodeNote : Json.Decode.Decoder Note`,
with `Json.Decode.decodeValue` or `Json.Decode.decodeString`, and rarely needs
these directly. The encoders are in [`Proto.Json.Encode`](Proto-Json-Encode).

Some names match elm/json's (`string`, `float`, `value`): every reference is
qualified, as `Proto.Json.Decode.float`, and these read proto3 JSON's forms,
which are wider than elm/json's (a number written as a string, `"NaN"`).


# Messages

A generated decoder checks once, with [`message`](#message), that the JSON is
an object, and then reads each field with a step that does not check it again.

@docs message, field, fieldEither, nullField, nullFieldEither, emptyMessage


# Messages that nest

A message that nests is read at most 100 levels deep. It nests when, through
its fields at any depth, it reaches a message on a cycle of references, which
can nest without bound, a `Value`, `Struct` or `ListValue`, or an `Any`. A
level is each such message entered, the outermost at 1. Its generated decoder
takes the depth, and reads each message it holds that nests one level deeper:

    decodeNode =
        decodeNestedNode 1

    decodeNestedNode =
        Proto.Json.Decode.memo
            (\depth ->
                Proto.Json.Decode.message
                    (Json.Decode.succeed Node
                        |> Proto.Json.Decode.field "children" [] (Json.Decode.list (Json.Decode.map Node_ (Json.Decode.lazy (\_ -> decodeNestedNode (depth + 1)))))
                    )
            )

A message on a cycle makes its decoder for each depth once, with
[`memo`](#memo), as above. A message on no cycle, whose depth the schema
bounds, has a function of the depth instead, and the message holding it reads
it with [`deeperLazy`](#deeperLazy). A message that holds an `Any` takes the
registry, so its decoder at a depth is built as the value is read, with
[`deeperLazy`](#deeperLazy) on a cycle and [`deeper`](#deeper) off one. Past
the limit a decoder fails with "Expecting messages nested at most 100 deep".

@docs memo, deeper, deeperLazy


# Oneofs

@docs oneof, members, Member, member, memberEither, nullMember, nullMemberEither


# Enums

@docs enum, enumOrZero


# Maps

@docs stringDict, int32Dict, uint32Dict, int64Dict, uint64Dict, boolMap


# Strings

@docs string


# 32-bit integers

@docs int32, uint32


# 64-bit integers

By default a 64-bit integer is held as its decimal string. Code generated with
the `int64=int` option, and fields marked `[jstype = JS_NUMBER]`, hold it as an
`Int` instead.

@docs int64, uint64
@docs int64AsInt, uint64AsInt


# Floating point

[`double`](#double) reads a `double` field, and [`float`](#float) a `float`
field, as [`Proto.Binary`](Proto-Binary) names them.

@docs double, float


# Bytes

By default a `bytes` field is held as its base64 text. Code generated with the
`bytes=bytes` option holds it as elm/bytes' `Bytes` instead; see
[`Proto.Bytes`](Proto-Bytes) for what `==` does with those.

@docs bytes
@docs bytesAsBytes


# Well-known types

`Value` is any JSON, held as a [`Proto.Value.Value`](Proto-Value#Value). A
message holding one nests, so it decodes with [`nestedValue`](#nestedValue):
a field of it through [`nullField`](#nullField), a oneof member
through [`nullMember`](#nullMember), and a list or map of it as any other
value. The `nested` forms read one held by a message
that nests, counting its levels on from the message's depth; [`value`](#value)
reads one on its own.
The wrapper types (`Int32Value` and the rest) use
their scalar's helpers.

@docs timestamp
@docs duration
@docs fieldMask
@docs empty
@docs nullValue
@docs struct, nestedStruct, value, nestedValue
@docs listValue, nestedListValue
@docs any, nestedAny, registryDepth

-}

